<DocsLayout
  docKey="export"
  title="Exporting"
  description="Saving a Deptheos render as a file: TIFF, PNG, JPEG or a linear DNG, bit depth, color profile, the metadata kept, file names and where exports go."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Exporting</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A render lives in the Library until you export it as a file for an editor, a print or the web. Export…
      (⌘E, or <em>Export</em> in the title bar) opens the Export sheet for the render on screen, retouching
      and <a href="/docs/adjust/">adjustments</a> included. From the Library, right-click a stack and choose Export…. Export Again (⇧⌘E) saves with the
      last choices and no sheet.
    </p>
  </div>
  <figure class="shot"><Shot name="export-dng" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Export sheet for a RAW CR3 stack with DNG selected, 16 bits per channel, and linear camera color." /><figcaption>The Export sheet for a RAW stack, with DNG chosen.</figcaption></figure>

  <section id="formats">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Format and bit depth</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="choose">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">Bits</th><th scope="col">Use it for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">TIFF</th><td>16 or 8</td><td>Editing in Photoshop, Affinity or GIMP. Lossless.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">PNG</th><td>16 or 8</td><td>Lossless too, and larger than TIFF.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">JPEG</th><td>8</td><td>Sharing. The quality is set in Settings ▸ Export (92 at first).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">DNG</th><td>16</td><td>RAW and DNG stacks only: a linear DNG, developed in your RAW editor like a RAW file.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <em>16 bits per channel</em> keeps 16 bits where the result has them: always for a RAW or DNG stack, and
        for a stack of standard images when any frame was 16-bit. A stack of 8-bit JPEGs has 8 bits of data
        either way. It is ticked or not as you last left it, in this sheet or the Batch dialog. The sheet starts on JPEG, or on DNG for a RAW or DNG stack, and after that on the format you last used
        for that kind of stack; <em>Settings ▸ Export</em> sets both. DNG is dimmed for a stack of JPEG, TIFF
        or PNG frames. <a href="/docs/raw/#keeps">RAW, DNG and color</a> explains what a DNG result
        keeps from the original files and what it gives up.
      </p>
      <p>
        TIFF compression is a setting, under Settings ▸ Export: ZIP (the default, the smallest files), LZW (read
        by the oldest software) or None (the fastest to write and open, and the largest). All three are
        lossless.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="color">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Color</h2>
      <p>The sheet's <em>Color</em> line says what the file's pixels will be:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>sRGB, like the frames</em>: standard frames in sRGB stay in sRGB.</li>
        <li>
          <em>Display P3, like the frames</em> (or another profile's name): frames in another color space keep
          it, and its profile is embedded in the file.
        </li>
        <li><em>sRGB, developed through the frames' camera profile</em>: a RAW stack saved as TIFF, PNG or JPEG.</li>
        <li><em>Linear camera color, with the frames' camera profile</em>: a RAW stack saved as DNG.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For a render with <a href="/docs/adjust/">adjustments</a>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A RAW render saved as TIFF, PNG or JPEG offers <em>sRGB</em>, <em>Display P3</em>, <em>Adobe RGB</em>
          or <em>ProPhoto RGB</em>, with its profile embedded. ProPhoto RGB is written with 16 bits only.
        </li>
        <li>A render of standard frames keeps their color space, unless their profile can't be kept with adjustments; then it is saved in sRGB, and the line says so.</li>
        <li><em>16 bits per channel</em> is offered for any adjusted render, even one of 8-bit frames.</li>
        <li>
          A DNG reads <em>Linear camera color; of the adjustments it carries only the white balance and the
          crop</em>: its pixels are as stacked, the white balance and Crop are settings your RAW editor reads.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If a render's saved adjustments are damaged or can't be read, its export stops with a notice rather than
        coming out without them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="metadata">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Metadata</h2>
      <p>
        An export copies the camera, lens, exposure settings and capture time of the frame the others were lined
        up to, along with the artist and copyright when the camera recorded them. It also records that Deptheos
        made it and the method used, and lists the file names of the stacked frames (names only, not their folders)
        in its XMP. GPS position, the owner's name, camera and lens serial numbers, maker notes and the focus
        distance are left out.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="naming">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Name and folder</h2>
      <p>
        The name starts as the stack's name and the render's number, such as <code>bee-3.tif</code>. Change the
        pattern under Settings ▸ Export ▸ <em>File name</em>, where <code>{"{stack}"}</code>,
        <code>{"{render}"}</code>, <code>{"{method}"}</code> and <code>{"{date}"}</code> are replaced and the rest stays as
        typed. Export never replaces a file: if one with that name is already in the folder, it adds a number,
        <code>bee-3-2.tif</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Export to</em> offers <em>Next to the frames</em> (the folder of the first frame), <em>Last folder
        used</em>, or <em>Choose…</em>. With <em>Show in Finder when done</em> ticked (<em>Show in Explorer when
        done</em> on Windows, <em>Show in File Manager when done</em> on Linux), the file is shown once it is saved; unticked, the notice that says it is saved has a
        <em>Show in Finder</em> button. Settings ▸ Export sets
        what the sheet starts with.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
